Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are gradually used since of their huge specific surface area and superior pore structure. However, there are problems such as easy aggregation and difficult separation in water treatment. In this study, we prepared composite microspheres (FMCS-1) by modifying MIL-125- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and chitosan. The structural characterization and performance analysis of the materials showed that the introduction of chi­ tosan effectively prevents the stacking of MOFs. The magnetic test manifested that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ solved the problem of the difficult separation of MOFs from water. The removal potential of toxic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was tested by adsorption experiments. The isotherm model indicated that FMCS-1 is a single molecular layer adsorbent with a maximum adsorption capacity of $109.46\mathrm{mg/g}$ at $\mathsf{p H}=2$ . The adsorption kinetics showed that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by FMCS-1 was chemical adsorption. The acid resistance test demonstrated that FMCS-1 can exist stably in acid solutions. The recycling experiments proved that the adsorbent can be reused and the removal percentage still reaches $50\%$ after 5 cycles. This work expands the application of MOFs in water treatment and also provides an effective adsorbent for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ removal.
## 1. Introduction
Water pollution by heavy metals in water has sparked widespread concern around the world [1]. Wastewater generated from numerous industries such as electroplating, manufacturing, wood preservation, and leather is the major source of Chromium $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , an emblematic pollutant [2]. $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ are two major forms among which the toxicity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ is about 100 times more toxic than Cr(III) [3]. More importantly, the highly mobile, non-degradable, and carcinogenic na­ ture of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in the environment may pose a serious health threat to humans and result in various issues like skin disease, liver damage, and genetic alteration [4]. In addition to easy inhalation of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from the environment through the respiratory tract, $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ is also accumulated in the body due to various normal activities of cells [5]. $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ has been identified as one of the three internationally recognized carcinogenic metals by the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) [6]. The World Health Organization (WHO) sets maximum limits of 0.05 $\mathrm{mg/L}$ for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in drinking water and $0.10~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ in surface water [7]. Therefore, in order to protect the ecological environment, the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from water assumes great significance. So far, photocatalysis, chemical precipitation, electrochemical, ion exchange, biological, and adsorption are the main methods for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ [8–13]. However, methods such as ion exchange and chemical precipitation require a large number of chemical reagents and can generate toxic substances in the process of treatment to cause secondary pollution [14]. Among the various methods, the adsorption method is widely used due to its simple operation, low operating cost, and generation of less sec­ ondary pollution [15,16]. The adsorption performance of conventional adsorbents such as zeolite, active carbon, and clays has been extensively studied. Therefore, in order to treat polluted water containing heavy metals and resourceful management of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , it is important to inves­ tigate novel adsorbents that can effectively separate $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in wastewater.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous materials consisting of metal ions/clusters and organic linkers [17]. MOFs are broadly applied in various fields including gas separation, drug delivery, and sensing as a result of their huge specific surface area, available pores, and large number of active sites [18–21]. MOFs have also found applications as adsorbents in water treatment in recent years. A large number of active sites on MOFs result in interactions with pollutants via hydrogen bonding, electrostatic interactions, and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, making MOFs an excellent candidate for adsorption [22]. Yu et al. confirmed by radial distribution function (RDF) that the formation of bonds with N atoms of the ligands with Fe in MIL-100(Fe) is the reason behind the removal of dodecylamine [23]. MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is one of the metal-organic frame­ works from the Materials Institute Lavoisier framework (MIL), having Ti (II) as the central metal ion [24]. $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ combines the advantage of MIL-125 and the amine group, giving it an abundance of reactive groups, high thermal and chemical stability, as well as homogeneous pores [25].
In spite of various advantages, MOFs also have practical disadvan­ tages. Due to the accumulation of most powdered MOFs, separation from the aqueous solutions is very difficult after the adsorption of con­ taminants and thus limiting their applications in the aqueous phase. In order to prevent agglomeration, MOFs can be modified into particles and membranes using formable materials while also increasing adsorption capacity. Chitosan (CS) is a biodegradable and low-cost product derived from the deacetylation of chitin and is one of the most abundant natural polymers in the environment [26]. Above all, the high proportion of amino and hydroxyl groups on the surface of chitosan renders it a superior adsorbent. The amino groups can be protonated under acidic conditions and adsorb negatively charged ions via elec­ trostatic interactions. In addition, the reactive functional groups on chitosan can also be functionalized by cross-linking with polymers, amination, and carboxymethylation to obtain composites with good properties [27,28]. In recent years, it was found that the composites of chitosan mixed with MOFs have satisfactory properties. Jeyaseelan and his colleagues were successful in resolving the issues of pressure drop and slow column filtering of powered MOFs in aqueous solutions due to aggregation of MOFs by creating hybrid beads via cross-linking
Al $@$ ABDC MOFs with chitosan [29].
The magnetic composites based on MOFs while improving the removal percentage of pollutants can also be effectively separated from the water. Li et al. prepared MOF-801 and a composite of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M O F}.$ 801 for the adsorption of $\mathsf{s e}(\mathrm{IV})$ in water [30]. The experimental results demonstrated that the removal of $95.2\ \%$ of Se(IV) under optimal con­ ditions by the material owing to the synergistic interaction between MOFs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ could be easily separated from the aqueous solutions. Shi et al. employed in-situ growth method to obtain $\mathrm{Cu-MOFs/Fe_{3}O_{4}}$ composites, and the obtained material showed much more effective adsorption of $\mathrm{{Pb(II)}}$ and malachite green (MG) compared to previous methods [31].
Based on the above discussion, we hypothesized that the novel magnetic composite (FMCS-1) formed by modifying MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and chitosan can effectively solve the rapid aggregation and separation problems of powdered MOFs’. Different means of charac­ terization were employed to explore the structure of the material FMCS1. The effectiveness of the composite for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was also assessed via batch adsorption studies. The adsorption mechanism of Cr (VI) by FMCS-1 was also discussed by employing a combination of adsorption models and characterization techniques. The possibility of practical applications of the composites was evaluated by carrying out desorption tests, acid stability experiments, and the effect of coexisting ions.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Chitosan (low viscosity, $<200~\mathrm{{mPa\cdots}}.$ ) was purchased from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd. Acetic acid, methanol, ethanol absolute, paraffin liquid, N, $N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), petroleum ether, potassium dichromate $(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}).$ ) were supplied by Guangdong Guanghua Sci-Tech Co., Ltd. Span-80 was provided by Tianjin Tianli Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., while $25\%$ glutaraldehyde solution was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. 2-Aminoterephthalic acid $(98~\%)$ , Titanium tetraisopropanolate $(97\ \%)$ were obtained from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was purchased from Tianjin Kemiou Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All the reagents used in this study were of analytical grade and were used without any further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of MIL-125- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$
A conventional solvothermal method was used to synthesize MIL$125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [32,33]. 2-Aminoterephthalic acid $(2.86~\mathrm{g})$ and titanium tet­ raisopropanolate $\ensuremath{(2.86~\mathrm{mL})}$ were dissolved in a mixture of $N_{\astrosun}$ , N-dime­ thylformamide (DMF) and methanol at room temperature. Subsequently, the homogeneously dispersed mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 16 h. After separation, the resultant yellow powder was washed several times with DMF and methanol. Eventually, the washed product was dried in an oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of FMCS
The composites were prepared using a previously reported method with certain modifications [34,35]. Briefly, an appropriate amount of chitosan was dissolved in $100\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid solution $(1\%,\mathrm{wt/v})$ . Then, $_{0.3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and a quantitative amount of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were well decentralized into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of the above solution using an ultrasonic cleaner. Afterwards, the mixture was poured into an emulsion system containing $50~\mathrm{mL}$ paraffin liquid and $4~\mathrm{mL}$ Span-80. 5 mL of $25\ \%$ glutaraldehyde solution was drop wise added into the system after the temperature reached $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then the mixture was stirred for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . In the following, $1\mathrm{MNaOH}$ solution $(5\mathrm{mL})$ was added to the mixture and then heated for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Thereafter an external magnet was used to separate the material. Finally, the obtained product was washed with petroleum ether and absolute ethanol for several times before being dried in the oven at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{h}$ . Three different composites based on different mass ratios of chitosan and MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (1.5:0.5, 1:1, 0.5:1.5) were syn­ thesized which were named as FMCS-1, FMCS-2, and FMCS-3. The synthesis flow diagram is shown in Fig. 1a.
## 2.4. Characterization
Scanning electron microscopic images (SEM) were recorded by a Zeiss Sigma300 equipped with an energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) to observe the surface morphology of the composite FMCS-1. Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopic (FTIR) measurement was carried out by Vertex 70 for analyzing the functional groups of FMCS-1. All the spectra were measured using pellets of KBr in the wavenumber range of 400 to $4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Besides, Bruker D8 Advance ( $\mathrm{\tilde{\Lambda}C u\ K d}$ radia­ tion) $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray spectrometer was used to measure the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of pristine and composite materials to understand the changes in crystallinity when the angle was scanned between $5^{\circ}-90^{\circ}$ . Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out by Thermo Sci­ entific K-Alpha for investigating the elemental composition of FMCS-1. Furthermore, the magnetic hysteresis loops of FMCS-1 were measured by VSM LakeShore7404 from $-20{,}000$ to 20,000 Oe both prior to and after adsorption. Zeta potential was determined by using a Malvern Nano Laser Particle Sizer (ZEN3600). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area, pore volume, and average pore size of the mate­ rials were analyzed at 77 K using a Quantachrome Autosorb IQ.
