## 2.6. Reusability
Reusability is a significant criterion for the practical application of absorbents. After loading $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , the composites were magnetically separated and immersed in $30\mathrm{mL}$ of 1 M NaOH solution and a mixture of methanol/NaCl solution ( $\mathrm{30~mL}$ ) for desorption $6\mathrm{h}$ , respectively. In the following, the materials were separated again for further reuse. The amount of residual $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ remaining in the solution after adsorption was determined by spectrophotometry at $540\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The experiments were repeated 5 times under the same steps and the reusability was analyzed by the results.
## 2.7. Acid resistance
Given the acidic nature of wastewater that contains heavy metals, the acid stability of the composites was investigated by immersing them in acid solutions of varying concentrations over a period of time. The FTIR spectra of about $10~\mathrm{mg}$ FMCS-1 were measured after shaking the material in different concentrations of HCl $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\zeta~}_{1}\mathrm{~M},0.1\mathrm{~M}$ , and 0.001 M) solutions at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of FMCS
The crystal structure of the samples was measured by studying the XRD patterns (Fig. 1b). The sample showed higher crystallinity indi­ cated by the sharper peak shape. The characteristic peaks of the as synthesized MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ appeared at $6.7^{\circ}$ , $9.7^{\circ}$ , $11.6^{\circ}$ , $15.3^{\circ}$ and $16.5^{\circ}$ corresponding to the crystal planes of (100), (200), (211), (103), and (222), respectively [36]. These sharp peaks indicated the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The amorphous structure of chitosan was reflected by the broad peaks in the XRD pattern. For the composite, a broad peak in the range of $13^{\circ}-22^{\circ}$ appeared shown in the upper right inset of Fig. 1b, confirming the presence of chitosan in the composite. In addition, the characteristic peaks of MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ could be observed in the composites. Therefore, the XRD patterns proved the successful preparation of FMCS-1 with MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , and chi­ tosan constituting the composite material.
The FTIR spectra of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ CS, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , FMCS-1, and FMCS- $^{1+}$ $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ were measured and are shown in Fig. 1c. For pristine MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , the detected characteristic peaks in the region of $1400{-}1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the vibrational bands corresponding to carbonyl asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations, which are typical vibrational bands [37]. The asymmetric and symmetric stretching vi­ brations of the $\tt N-H$ bond appeared at $3439\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3363\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [38]. Moreover, the adsorption bands at $400{-}800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the Ti–O–Ti band while the band at $1256\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to the $\mathsf{C-N}$ symmetric stretching vibration of the benzene ring [39]. These statistics demonstrated the successful synthesis of MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . In the case of CS, the peak at $3425~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the vibrations of O–H and N–H bonds [40]. Two peaks appearing at $2925~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2859~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibration peaks of aliphatic $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ , respectively [41]. Additionally, the respective stretching vibrations of the N–H and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ are observed around $1646~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ and $1324\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [42]. The peaks located at $1031\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1085\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be assigned to the ${\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{O H}}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{O H}}$ bonds, respectively [43]. For $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , the stretching vibration of Fe–O was detected at $578\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which in the composite appeared at $548~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [44]. In comparison to MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and chitosan, the appearance of a large peak at $1713~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corre­ sponding to the $-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}$ bond in the prepared composite FMCS-1 in­ dicates a successful reaction of the amino groups on chitosan and MIL$125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ with the aldehyde groups of glutaraldehyde through Schiff base reaction to obtain the composite microspheres [45].
In the acid resistance test, it is evident from the FTIR results (Fig. 1d) that the main characteristic peaks of the adsorbents remained after immersion in different concentrations of acid. This indicated the good acid stability of FMCS-1 implying the suitability of the composite for practical application in water treatment.
A vibrating sample magnetometer was used to measure the magnetic properties of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and FMCS-1 before and after the adsorption of Cr (VI). As shown in Fig. 1e, the respective saturation magnetic value of the synthesized FMCS-1 was $3.56\mathrm{\emu{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ and $3.24\mathrm{\emu{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ before and after the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . These values were significantly lower than that of the pure $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}(78.30\mathrm{emucdotg^{-1}})$ , which is mainly due to the presence of non-magnetic chitosan and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the sample. Nonetheless, it was still possible to effectively separate the composite from aqueous solutions after adsorption by an external magnetic field (as shown in the upper right inset of Fig. 1e). In addition, the super­ paramagnetic nature of the adsorbent is shown by the nearly zero values of the remanence magnetization (Mr) and coercivity field (Hc). The VSM results of the composites are coherent with the above results, which is also good proof of the successful preparation of the composite and its
potential in practical applications.
The surface morphology of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and FMCS-1 were investi­ gated from the SEM micrographs of the samples. The prepared MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was a truncated bipyramid octahedron with a good structure and more uniform size having a large amount of MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ stacked up as shown in Fig. 2(a, b). In comparison, the composite showed relatively smooth microspheres with an average diameter of about $41.16~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ as can be seen in Fig. 2(c, d). At the same time, encapsulation of chitosan resulted in strong dispersibility of the composite material, which not only avoided the stacking of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ efficiently but also resulted in enhanced contact area with pollutants.
In addition, the elemental composition and distribution on the sur­ face of the composite after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ were explored by EDS (Fig. 2e–l). According to the SEM-EDS mapping spectra of the composite after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , the elements C, N, Ti, Fe, and Cr were found to be distributed on the surface of composites with the respective pro­ portions of $48.91\ \%$ , $37.82\ \%$ , $4.73\ \%$ , $0.76~\%$ , and $7.78\ \%$ . This conclusively proved that the composite was composed of MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , and chitosan, which is consistent with the XRD results. Further­ more, it also proved the uniform adsorption of Cr on the composite.
Changes in the pore structure of the composites before and after the adsorption of Cr were measured via ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption experi­ ments. As can be seen in Table S1, the surface area, pore volume, and average pore diameter of the composites were $2.26~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 0.00055 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and $17.628~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The introduction of non-porous chitosan resulted in a smaller surface area of the material. After loading of Cr, the surface area and the pore volume of Cr-loaded FMCS-1 had decreased. This is mainly due to the blockage of some pores after adsorption.
## 3.2. Adsorption of FMCS for Cr(VI)
To compare the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ adsorption performance of MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CS, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , FMCS-1, FMCS-2, and FMCS-3, the same quantity of material was placed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of $50\mathrm{mg/LCr(VI)}$ solution at $303\mathrm{K}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . As can be seen in Fig. 3a, the maximum adsorption capacity and removal per­ centage of the FMCS-1 were $109.46~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $72.98~\%$ , respectively. This may be due to (1) the presence of numerous active sites on the FMCS-1; (2) and uniform dispersion of the FMCS-1 in water and a large contact area with heavy metal ions in the solution. Based on the above results, FMCS-1 was selected for subsequent experiments.
## 3.2.1. Dosage
The variation of adsorption capacity and removal percentage with the change in dosage of the composite FMCS-1 was increased from $_{0.005\mathrm{~g~}}$ to $0.040~\mathrm{g}$ and is shown in Fig. 3b. When the dosage of FMCS-1 was $<0.010\ g$ , the adsorption capacity was high but the removal per­ centage was low. This was mainly due to the limited amount of adsor­ bent, resulting in the saturation of the adsorption sites. As the increasing dosage of adsorbent, the removal percentage increased accompanied by a sharp decrease in the adsorption capacity. This was primarily because of more active sites due to the increasing number of adsorbents, but it was accompanied by the aggregation of active sites, resulting in the decreased effective utilization rate of the active sites. Interestingly, the removal percentage of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was close to $100\%$ when the amount of adsorbent was increased to $\phantom{-}0.040\ g$ , exhibiting strong adsorption per­ formance of FMCS-1 for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . The optimum amount of adsorbent was selected as $0.010{\mathrm{~g~}}$ in the subsequent experiments, in combination with the adsorption capacity and resource utilization.
## 3.2.2. pH
The pH of the solution is an influencing factor on the adsorption capacity of an adsorbent due to its effect on the surface charge of the adsorbent as well as the ionic species of the contaminants. Depending on the solution pH, the species of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ varied with $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}$ are the existing forms of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in the pH range of 2 to 6 while $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ is the predominant species when $\mathrm{pH}>6$ [46]. Fig. 3c illustrates the adsorption behavior of the composites towards $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ at various $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ . A sharp decrease in the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was observed for FMCS-1 from $109.46~\mathrm{mg/g}$ to $9.34~\mathrm{mg/g}$ when the $\mathfrak{p H}$ was increased from 2.0 to 9.0, with the removal percentage decreasing from $72.98\%$ to $6.23\%$ . From the Zeta potential plots shown in Fig. 3d, the $\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}}$ of FMCS-1 was found to be 2.15. As a direct consequence of the increasing number of reactive groups $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ in FMCS-1 due to the presence of chitosan, a huge proportion of amino groups on the FMCS-1 undergo protonation and become positively charged after binding $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ in the solution when the $\mathrm{pH}<2.15$ . This positive charge surface undergoes electrostatic attrac­ tion with the negatively charged $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions in the solution. At high pH, deprotonation of the reactive groups results in electrostatic repulsion between $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ and FMCS-1 and thereby decreases the adsorption ca­ pacity. Moreover, the competitive adsorption of negatively charged -OH with $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in alkaline conditions is unfavorable for the adsorption of Cr (VI) by FMCS-1. Thus, in all further studies solution pH was maintained at 2.
## 3.2.3. Contact time
With varying contact time, the adsorption capacity of the composites at various concentrations of Cr(VI) $(20{-}50~\mathrm{mg/L})$ changes, as shown in Fig. 4a. At the initial stage of contact, the rapid increase of the adsorp­ tion capacity of FMCS-1 towards $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was due to the fact that there were enough surface active sites on FMCS-1 which are attractive to pollutants along with a large concentration gradient between the adsorbent and the contaminant [47]. With increasing contact time, the reaction was gradually balanced as the adsorption sites are gradually occupied by $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . Equilibrium was achieved for the adsorption of Cr (VI) by FMCS-1 after $360\mathrm{{min}}$ due to the decrease of available active sites and concentration gradient of the contaminant.
## 3.3. Adsorption kinetics
Adsorption kinetics is primarily investigated to identify the factors influencing the rate of adsorption by modeling the relationship between adsorption capacity and time during the adsorption process. Therefore, the adsorption of the composites at different concentrations of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ solutions $(20{-}50~\mathrm{mg/L})$ was set up and the data were fitted using the pseudo-first-order model (Eq. (3)) and the pseudo-second-order model (Eq. (4)).
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\displaystyle{l n(q_{e}-q_{t})=l n q_{e}-k_{1}t}}\\ {\displaystyle{\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}}}\end{array}
$$
Here ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}}\left({\bf m}{\bf g}/{\bf g}\right)$ represents the adsorption capacity of FMCS-1 at time $\mathbf{t},\mathbf{k}_{1}\ (\mathbf{min}^{-1})$ and $\mathbf{k}_{2}~(\mathbf{g}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{i}\mathbf{n}^{-1})$ ) represent the corresponding rate constants.
According to the fitting results shown in Fig. 4b, c, and Table 1, the adsorption process of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ on the FMCS-1 under different concentra­ tions could be described more suitably using the pseudo-second-order model. All the correlation coefficients $(\mathrm{R}^{2}\ =\ 0.997–0.999)$ of the Pseudo-second-order model are higher and the theoretical maximum adsorption capacities $(\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e,cal}})$ are also closer to the experimental data $\mathsf{(q_{e_{i}}}$ $_\mathrm{exp})$ . In addition, the chi-square $(\chi^{2})$ of the pseudo-second-order model is smaller than the pseudo-first-order model. Hence, the adsorption of $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) by the FMCS-1 is dominated by chemical adsorption, which in­ volves electron exchange [48].
To further probe the adsorption process, the data were fitted by the intraparticle diffusion model (Eq. (5)) [49].
$$
q_{t}=K_{i d}t^{0.5}+C
$$
Here, $\mathrm{K_{id}\ (m g{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}m i n^{0.5})}$ represents the rate constant, and C is a constant.
## 3.4. Adsorption isotherms
The adsorption isotherm principally discusses the surface properties of the material and its affinity for the contaminant. The Langmuir model assumes that the monolayer adsorption takes place on a homogeneous adsorbent surface where the active sites are energetically identical and there are no interactions between the adsorbates, whereas the Freund­ lich model is used to describe the adsorption process that occurs on nonuniform surfaces [51,52]. The Temkin model describes a multilayer chemisorption process, and the D-R model assumes a multilayer struc­ ture and is suitable for physical adsorption by van Der Waal’s forces [53]. Therefore, the Langmuir model equations (Eqs. (6) and (7)), the Freundlich model equations (Eq. (8)), the Temkin model equations (Eq. (9)), and Dubinin-Radushkevich model (Eqs. (10) and (11)) were used to fit the adsorption capacities obtained at different temperatures (293 K–303 K) for different $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentrations $(20{-}50~\mathrm{mg/L})$ .
$$
{\frac{C_{e}}{q_{e}}}={\frac{C_{e}}{q_{m}}}+{\frac{1}{q_{m}k_{L}}}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{l}{R_{L}=\displaystyle\frac{1}{1+K_{L}C_{0}}}\\ {q_{e}=k_{\mathrm{F}}C_{e}^{\mathrm{i}m}}\\ {q_{e}=\displaystyle\frac{R T_{\mathit{h}}}{b}{I_{\mathit{h}}}(K_{\mathit{c}}{c}_{e})}\\ {{h q_{e}=h q_{e n}-K_{n n}\varepsilon^{2}}}\\ {\displaystyle}\\ {\displaystyle\varepsilon=R T{I_{0}}\left(1+\frac{1}{C_{e}}\right)}\end{array}
$$
with the previous results. Therefore, the composite material that absorbs $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ is monolayer adsorption on a homogeneous surface where the active sites are energetically identical and there are no interactions be­ tween the adsorbates. Additionally, the absorbent in this study was compared with the materials reported in the published literature for the adsorption of $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ . The produced composite is an effective absorbent for removing $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ pollution from water, as shown in Table 2.
## 3.5. Adsorption thermodynamics
At various temperatures, the thermodynamic parameters of adsorp­ tion were estimated to verify the findings of the adsorption isotherms by carrying out adsorption experiments at different temperatures $(293~\mathrm{K},$ $303\mathrm{~K},\ 313\mathrm{~K})$ . Gibbs free energy (ΔG), entropy change (ΔS) and enthalpy change (ΔH) are three primary adsorption kinetic parameters that are computed using Eqs. (12)–(14) [63]:
Here, ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{m}}~(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}{\bf g}^{-1})$ represents the theoretical maximum adsorption capacity of FMCS-1. $\mathrm{K_{L}}$ $\left(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\right)$ , n, and $\mathrm{s_{F}}(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ represent the Langmuir and Freundlich constant. b is the Temkin isotherm constant. T is the adsorption temperature (K). $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{DR}}(\mathbf{mol}^{2}{\cdot}(\mathbf{kJ}^{2})^{-1})$ )and ε represent the constants of the D-R model.
As illustrated in Fig. 5a, when the solution temperature rose, the adsorption capacity of the FMCS-1 also increased. This could be due to the high temperature causing frequent movement of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions, which increases the probability of contacting the composites. According to the fitting results in Table S4, the Langmuir model could explain the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by the composite materials more accurately than other isotherm models as indicated by higher values of the correlation coefficient of the Langmuir model $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.997–0.998)$ . The chi-square $(\chi^{2})$ in the Langmuir model was also smaller than in other models. In addition, the coefficient $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ of the Langmuir model (0.0578–0.960) was between 0 and 1 proving that the FMCS-1 has a strong affinity towards $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ [54]. The mean adsorption energy (E) calculated by the D-R model was also higher than $8\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , indicating the adsorption of Cr (VI) on FMCS-1 was mainly chemical adsorption, which is consistent
$$
K={\frac{q_{e}}{C_{e}}}
$$
$$
\Delta G=-R T l n K
$$
$$
l n K=\frac{\Delta S}{R}-\frac{\Delta H}{R T}
$$
Here, T (K) represents the thermodynamic temperature and R (8.314 $\mathbf{J/mol{\cdot}K})$ represents the gas constant.
The thermodynamic parameters of adsorption are presented in Table S5. The negative $\Delta G$ values at various temperatures implied that $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ adsorption by the composite FMCS-1 occurred spontaneously. Meanwhile, increasing temperature resulted in a gradual decrease of ΔG, indicating that the adsorption process could be promoted at higher temperature, which was consistent with the previous findings. The positive $\Delta\mathrm{H}$ values $(29.825~\mathrm{{\kJ}{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}.$ ) suggested that the entire adsorption process was heating-absorbing, which is another way to explain the adsorption isotherm data. An optimistic value of ΔS denoted that the disorder of the solid-liquid interface intensified during the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by FMCS-1. This was most likely caused by the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ as well as the accelerated movement of molecules after heat adsorption.
## 3.6. Desorption cycles and cost analysis
From an economic standpoint, by increasing material utilization adsorbents with regenerative properties can significantly increase material utilization and save funds. The results indicated that the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by the FMCS-1 decreased from $72.98~\%$ to $50.51\ \%$ after 5 cycles (Fig. 6a). Similarly, after five cycles, the desorption per­ centage of the composite using methanol/NaCl solution was reduced by approximately $10\%$ from $71.67\%$ to $61.45\%$ (Fig. S1). This indicated some of the active sites were still occupied by heavy metal ions after desorption resulting in a reduction of the binding sites. The adsorption capacity of FMCS-1 after 5 cycles still reaches about $50\%$ . This indicates that the recyclable performance of FMCS-1 is acceptable, with potential industrial applications.
The cost analysis is also meaningful for the adsorbent. The cost of raw materials for the composite FMCS-1 was calculated in Table S6. The electricity cost was not calculated since the electricity consumed by various production equipment is different. Except for electricity, the raw material price of $_{1g}$ composite is about ${5.3413\S}$ . Therefore, FMCS-1 is economically promising in practical applications.
## 3.7. Coexisting ions
In general, the presence of numerous coexisting ions in the effluent may hamper the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by the absorbent. Various anions $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2^{-}}$ , $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ ) and cations $(\mathrm{Na^{+},K^{+},C a^{2+}})$ were added into the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ solution separately for investigating the effect of coex­ isting ions on the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by the FMCS-1 (Fig. 6b). Given the electrostatic repulsion between the positively charged composite and coexisting cations at lower $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ , cations $\mathrm{(Na^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathsf{C a^{2+}}$ ) had no impact on the adsorption of $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ . Since $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ are low-affinity anions, they had a lower impact on the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ compared to that of the other coexisting anions. On the contrary, the strong affinity of $s0_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ to form inner complexes with the FMCS-1 may be the cause of their significant competitive effect on the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in the presence of oxygen anions, resulting in the occupation of some of the active sites and ultimately leading to the reduction of adsorption capacity.
## 3.8. Adsorption mechanism
To gain insight into the adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by FMCS-1, the FTIR and XPS spectra of FMCS-1 were measured before and after the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . The FTIR peak at $3423~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for FMCS-1 after Cr (VI) adsorption shifted to $3370\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while the peaks at $1061~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1034~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ combined into a single peak at $1061~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , indicating that the involvement of $\scriptstyle{0-\mathrm{H}}$ on the FMCS-1 in the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ through hydrogen bonding. At the same time, slight right shifting of the characteristic band of the $\tt N-H$ from $1634~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1625\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ revealed the involvement of amino groups from FMCS-1 in the adsorption. In addition, the appearance of a new peak at $887\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by the FMCS-1 is indicative of the formation of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI}){-}0$ bonding [64].
XPS characterization (Fig. 7) indicated the changes in the chemical environment for C, N, and Cr in the FMCS-1 after adsorption, which is helpful for explaining the adsorption mechanism in a better way. The appearance of $\mathtt{C r2p}$ peaks in the survey spectrum (Fig. 7a) indicates the successful adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . From the comparison of Fig. 7c and d, the peak position of -NH in the N1s spectrum before and after adsorption changed from $401.48\ \mathrm{eV}$ to $398.98\mathrm{~eV}$ , while that of ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ decreased from $398.88\mathrm{~\eV}$ to 397.78 eV, which were also accompanied by a decrease of content from $48.87\%$ and $51.13\%$ to $8.79\%$ and $25.95\%$ , respectively, indicating the involvement of -NH and $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ in the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . This includes two main aspects: (1) appearance of a new peak at $401.08\ \mathrm{eV}$ after adsorption indicative of the presence of - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ , implying protonation of the amino group on the FMCS-1 under acidic conditions during the adsorption process [65]; (2) N can also provide electrons for reducing $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ [66]. In the highresolution spectrum of $\mathtt{C r2p}$ (Fig. 7b), the peaks at $576.48\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 586.45 eV correspond to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ , while the peaks at $577.78\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 587.61 eV correspond to $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ [67,68], indicating the partial reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ [69]. Therefore, the adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ on FMCS-1 can be divided into three parts: hydrogen bonding, electro­ static attraction, and reduction, as shown in Fig. 8.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, we prepared composite adsorbents by modifying MIL$125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with chitosan and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The successful preparation of the composite FMCS-1 was confirmed by FTIR, XRD, SEM-EDS, Zeta, and VSM. The effects of different parameters like adsorbent dosage, pH, and concentration of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in the solution on the adsorption process were investigated. Under the condition of adsorbent dosage of $_{0.01\ g}$ , initial $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration of $50\mathrm{mg/L}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{pH}}=2$ , and the maximum adsorption capacity in accordance with the pseudo-second-order kinetic model was $109.46\mathrm{mg/g}$ . The adsorption process was chemisorption dominated by electron exchange. The spontaneous and endothermic nature of the adsorption process was indicated by the thermodynamic parameters. FMCS-1 has favorable acid stabilization and recyclable performance. Therefore, the experimental results confirm that our method is feasible and that the composite FMCS-1 in an efficient and easily separable adsorbent has the potential for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
